#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:11
msgid "Flocs, Fractals, Forces"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:13
msgid "The physics of flocs, fractals, fluids, and forces define the processes of flocculation, floc filters, lamellar sedimentation, and filters. The chemical bonds that hold flocs together determine the maximum diameter that a floc can grow to in the fluid shear of a flocculator. The force of gravity is strong enough to break flocs and determines the maximum size that a floc can grow to on planet earth or, for that matter, in the floc filter that forms in the bottom of the AguaClara clarifiers. The maximum diameter that a floc can grow to without being pulled apart by gravity sets a maximum terminal velocity for flocs and that has direct implications for the maximum upflow velocity than can be used in upflow clarifiers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:15
msgid "The three forces of chemical bonds, gravity, and shear can be grouped into two dimensionless parameters that will define the relationships between floc size, floc density, and these forces."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:18
msgid "Characteristic Core Particle"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:20
msgid "The concept of a characteristic core particle that sets the floc properties as it moves through the process of the flocculation, floc filter, settlers, and filter arose with two AguaClara plants in Honduras that ran into difficulties treating water from pristine high elevation mountain water sources. In both cases the water was very low turbidity and was yellow from dissolved organics. The exact source of the color was not identified, but it was likely the decaying leaf litter from the forests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:22
msgid "The high concentration of dissolved organics in the raw water requires a significant dosage of coagulant and there is very little clay present. The result is a very low density floc made of organic matter that is close to the density of water and perhaps even less dense than water and coagulant nanoparticles. These low density flocs are not captured efficiently by the plate settlers. The challenge of treating these two water sources led to the insight that we could characterize the properties of a floc if we understood the characteristic density and size of the core particle that aggregates to form the flocs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:24
msgid "The algorithm to define the characteristic density and diameter of the core particle is still under development. Nonetheless, the density and diameter of the characteristic core particle that aggregates to form the flocs are the key parameters that determine the performance of each stage of the treatment process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:28
msgid "Fractal Flocs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:30
msgid "Flocs are fractals. Fractals describe structures in which similar patterns occur over a range of scales. During flocculation flocs grow larger and larger by combining similar sized flocs. Flocs do not combine like raindrops! As raindrops combine they conserve both volume and mass.  With each subsequent collision the floc incorporates more water and thus its density gradually approaches the density of water. The inclusion of more and more water as the floc grows means that the floc mass is NOT conserved as it grows. Likewise, the volume of the floc is not conserved as it grows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:32
msgid "When :math:`n_{raindrops}` of equal volume :math:`\\forall_0` combine the resulting volume is :math:`\\forall = \\forall_0 n_{raindrops}`. When flocs combine the equation is slightly more complicated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:34
msgid "\\forall_{floc} = \\forall_{cp} n_{cp}^\\frac{3}{\\Pi_{fractal}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:39
#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:58
msgid "Where"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:40
msgid ":math:`\\forall_{floc}` is the floc volume"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:41
#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:60
msgid ":math:`n_{cp}` is the number of core particles in the floc"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:42
msgid ":math:`\\forall_{cp}` is the core particle volume"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:43
#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:62
msgid ":math:`\\Pi_{fractal}` is the floc volume-based fractal dimension"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:45
msgid "Raindrops conserve volume when they collide and have a :math:`\\Pi_{fractal}` of 3. If a particle would conserve its projected area when it combines, then it would have a :math:`\\Pi_{fractal}` of 2. If a particle would conserve its diameter when it combines, then it would have a :math:`\\Pi_{fractal}` of 1. A fractal dimension of 1 could occur if the particles all attached into a single long chain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:47
msgid "The fractal dimension of the resulting floc is a function of the difference in size of the colliding particles. We will show in the chapter on flocculation that the aggregation of flocs in a shear environment is limited to flocs that are of similar size. The sequential collisions of similar sized particles is the basis of the hierarchical model described by `Meakin (1988) <https://www-sciencedirect-com.proxy.library.cornell.edu/science/article/pii/0001868687800167>`_. The fractal dimension of flocs that form from collisions of similarly sized flocs has a slightly lower value than would be obtained from collisions between differently sized flocs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:49
msgid "The fractal dimension of flocs formed through hierarchical (same size) collisions has been bracketed between about 1.89 and 2.13. The fractal dimension of 1.89 is the result of aggregation where the flocs only attach at one point and do not rotate and make additional attachments after the initial collision. `Meakin (1988) <https://www-sciencedirect-com.proxy.library.cornell.edu/science/article/pii/0001868687800167>`_ simulated floc cluster aggregation that considered potential relative rotation of the two flocs and the formation of additional attachment points. After the initial collision with one attachment point the flocs rotate until they collide again and make a second attachment. The two attachment points will still allow rotation about an axis defined by the two attachment points. The flocs can rotate about that axis until a 3rd point of attachment is formed. With 3 points of attachment the relative motion of the 2 flocs is completely constrained."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:51
msgid "The fractal dimension increases with each additional attachment point. `Meakin (1988) <https://www-sciencedirect-com.proxy.library.cornell.edu/science/article/pii/0001868687800167>`_ found  fractal dimensions :math:`\\Pi_{fractal}` of 1.89, 2.08, and 2.13 were obtained for initial contact, rotation until contact in a direction that decreases the distance between the centers of mass of the two flocs, and then rotation about the axis defined by the two contact points in a direction that would again move their centers of mass to be closer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:53
msgid "D_{floc} = D_{cp} n_{cp}^\\frac{1}{\\Pi_{fractal}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:59
msgid ":math:`D_{floc}` is the floc diameter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:61
msgid ":math:`D_{cp}` is the core particle diameter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:64
msgid "The number of core particles in a floc is obtained by solving Equation :eq:`D_floc_of_n_cp` for :math:`n_{cp}`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:66
msgid "n_{cp} = \\left(\\frac{D_{floc}}{D_{cp}}\\right)^{\\Pi_{fractal}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:72
msgid "Floc Density"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:74
msgid "The density of a floc decreases as it grows larger. The density of the floc can be calculated based on conservation of mass and volume. Note that as the floc grows it incorporates more and more water and thus the initial mass of the core particles does not equal the final mass of the larger flocs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:76
msgid "M_{mix} = M_1 + M_2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:81
msgid "Mass conservation can be written in terms of density as:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:83
msgid "\\rho_{mix} \\forall_{mix} =\n"
"\\rho_1 \\forall_1 + \\rho_2 \\forall_2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:89
msgid "Written in terms of water and core particles the floc mass conservation and taking into account that the water volume is equal to the floc volume minus the core particle volume we obtain"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:91
msgid "\\rho_{floc} \\forall_{floc} =\n"
"\\rho_{H_2O} \\forall_{floc} - \\rho_{H_2O}\\forall_{n_{cp}} + \\rho_{cp} \\forall_{n_{cp}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:97
msgid "where :math:`\\forall_{n_{cp}}` is the total volume of the core particles in the floc. The buoyant density is the parameter of interest when calculating terminal velocity and thus Equation :eq:`floc_mass_conservation` can be rewritten as"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:99
msgid "\\left( \\rho_{floc} - \\rho_{H_2O} \\right)  =\n"
"\\left( \\rho_{cp}  - \\rho_{H_2O} \\right) \\frac{\\forall_{n_{cp}}}{ \\forall_{floc}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:105
msgid "The floc volume can be rewritten as a function of the floc diameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:107
msgid "\\forall_{floc} =\n"
"\\frac{\\pi}{6} D_{floc}^3"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:113
msgid "The volume of core particles in the floc is a function of the number of core particles."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:115
msgid "\\forall_{n_{cp}} =\n"
"n_{cp} \\frac{\\pi}{6} D_{cp}^3"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:121
msgid "The number of core particles can be eliminated from Equation :eq:`volume_core_particles_of_D_1` by substituting Equation :eq:`n_cp_of_D_floc`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:123
msgid "\\forall_{n_{cp}} =\n"
"\\frac{\\pi}{6} D_{cp}^3 \\left(\\frac{D_{floc}}{D_{cp}}\\right)^{\\Pi_{fractal}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:130
msgid "Substituting the floc volume and the core particle volume into Equation :eq:`floc_buoyant_density_1`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:132
msgid "\\left( \\rho_{floc} - \\rho_{H_2O} \\right)  =\n"
"\\left( \\rho_{cp}  - \\rho_{H_2O} \\right) \\frac{\\frac{\\pi}{6} D_{cp}^3\n"
"\\left(\\frac{D_{floc}}{D_{cp}}\\right)^{\\Pi_{fractal}} }{ \\frac{\\pi}{6} D_{floc}^3}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:139
msgid "Simplifying :eq:`floc_buoyant_density_2` we obtain"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:141
msgid "\\left( \\rho_{floc} - \\rho_{H_2O} \\right)  =\n"
"\\left( \\rho_{cp}  - \\rho_{H_2O} \\right)\n"
"\\left(\\frac{D_{cp}}{D_{floc}}\\right)^{3-\\Pi_{fractal}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:148
msgid "For volume-based fractal dimension less than 3 the buoyant density decreases as the floc size increases. The smaller the fractal dimension the less dense the resulting floc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:150
msgid "The floc density assuming a fractal dimension of 2 is given by"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:152
msgid "\\rho_{floc} = \\rho_{H_2O} + \\left( \\rho_{cp}  - \\rho_{H_2O} \\right) \\left(\\frac{D_{cp}}{D_{floc}}\\right)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:160
msgid "Floc Terminal Velocity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:162
msgid "As flocs settle in water, they will fall at a speed dictated by the weight of the floc, the buoyancy of the floc, and drag from the water. These three forces - the gravitational weight force, the buoyant force, and the drag force - dictate the speed at which a floc falls and are detailed in the free body diagram of :numref:`figure_terminal_velocity_FBD`. We care about determining the speed at which flocs will fall because knowing this information will help inform plate settler design."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:171
msgid "Buyouant force, drag force, and gravitational force shown for floc free body diagram."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:171
msgid "Free body diagram of a floc in water."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:173
msgid "To determine the force balance on a falling floc, consider:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:175
msgid "\\sum F = m a"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:179
msgid "At terminal velocity, the floc has been falling for a long period of time so there is no acceleration and the right side of the equation simplifies to zero."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:181
msgid ":math:`F_{drag} + F_{buoyant} - W_{floc} = 0`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:183
msgid "The drag force is determined by:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:185
msgid "F_{drag} = C_D A_{floc} \\rho_{H_2O} \\frac{v_t^2}{2}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:190
msgid "The buoyant force is given by:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:192
msgid "F_{buoyant} = \\forall_{floc} \\rho_{H_2O} g"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:197
msgid "The weight of the sphere is given by:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:199
msgid "W_{floc} = \\forall_{floc} \\rho_{floc} g"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:204
#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:293
msgid "Where:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:205
msgid ":math:`\\forall_{floc} =` floc particle volume"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:206
msgid ":math:`A_{floc} =` particle projected cross-sectional area"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:207
msgid ":math:`\\rho_{floc} =` particle density"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:208
msgid ":math:`\\rho_{H_2O} =` water density"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:209
msgid ":math:`g =` acceleration due to gravity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:210
msgid ":math:`C_D =` drag coefficient"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:211
msgid ":math:`v_t=` particle terminal velocity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:212
msgid ":math:`D=` particle diameter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:214
msgid "Plugging into the original force balance,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:216
msgid "\\left [C_D A_{floc} \\rho_{H_2O} \\frac{v_t^2}{2} \\right]+\\left [\\forall_{floc} \\rho_{H_2O} g\\right ]-\\left [\\forall_{floc} \\rho_{floc} g \\right] =0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:220
#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:749
msgid "Solving for terminal velocity, :math:`v_t`, we obtain"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:222
msgid "v_t = \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{3}\\frac{g D_{floc}}{C_D}\\frac{(\\rho_{floc}-\\rho_{H_2O})}{\\rho_{H_2O}}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:227
msgid "Terminal velocity is a function of fluid density, floc density, gravity, particle diameter, and the drag coefficient. To calculate velocity, all of those inputs must be determined."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:229
msgid "The first component that we will focus on is the drag coefficient, :math:`C_D`. The drag coefficient is function of Reynolds Number, :math:`Re`, and the characteristic flow around a particle. As a reminder, :math:`Re = \\frac{v_t D_{floc}}{\\nu}` where :math:`v_t` is the velocity of the fluid relative to the particle, :math:`D_{floc}` is the characteristic length scale (in this case the floc diameter), and :math:`\\nu` is kinematic viscosity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:231
msgid "Drag coefficients are used to describe flow around a particle and are shown in :numref:`figure_drag_coeff_Re_base`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:240
#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:266
msgid "Drag coefficient as a function of Reynolds number."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flocs_Fractals_and_Forces/FFF_Intro.rst:240
msgid "Drag coefficient on a sphere as a function of Reynolds number."
msgstr ""

